---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About - Web Performance KB"
  description="A demo Astro + MDX authority site with strict Zod validation, inline citations, and JSON-LD structured data.">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 prose-article">
    <h1>About this site</h1>
    <p>
      This is a demo Astro authority site built to showcase a content-and-data
      pipeline with strict, build-time validation. Every article is validated
      against a Zod schema, every citation is cross-checked against a registry,
      and the build fails loudly on any malformed input.
    </p>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Content layer:</strong> Astro Content Collections with Zod-validated frontmatter.</li>
      <li><strong>Citation engine:</strong> a single JSON registry, validated once at module load.</li>
      <li><strong>Structured data:</strong> JSON-LD generated from the same object the page renders.</li>
      <li><strong>Performance budget:</strong> Lighthouse CI gates 95+ on every push.</li>
      <li><strong>Hosting:</strong> Cloudflare Pages, static-only, no runtime.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why fail-fast validation</h2>
    <p>
      Editorial sites rot in invisible ways: stale frontmatter, broken citations,
      missing fields. The cheapest place to catch these is the build. If a
      reference is malformed, the build refuses to ship.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
